# saneland — auto-maximize policy for THIS machine. Git-ignored.
#
# Read before the tracked window-policy.conf, and first match wins, so
# anything here overrides a shipped default. Same format:
#
#     always   maximize on open      leave   keep the size it asked for
#     never    don't touch it at all, and never remember its geometry
#
# You rarely need to edit this by hand. Focus a window and press mod+Alt+m to
# flip its class between `always` and `leave`; `hypr-window-policy always`,
# `... leave`, `... never` and `... forget` do the same from a shell, and
# `hypr-window-policy list` prints the effective table.
#
# Two things worth knowing before you add rules:
#
#   * You may not need one. Sizes are remembered per class — resize a window
#     once and it comes back that way. A rule is for the FIRST launch, before
#     there is anything to remember.
#   * Overriding a family works: the tracked file maximizes browsers as a
#     group, and `leave ^chromium$` here exempts one of them.

# Example — a file manager you want full-screen, and one app from a family
# you'd rather keep small:
#
# always  ^org\.gnome\.Nautilus$
# leave   ^vlc$

# Lines the daemon writes itself land under this heading: when a maximize is
# refused by the client (a fixed-size dialog silently keeps its own size),
# the class is demoted to `leave` so it stops trying. It will not overwrite a
# rule you wrote — an explicit choice of yours always stands.
